"From an airplane or a satellite, Taiwan's main island looks like a long spindle, wide in the middle and narrow at both ends. According to the Executive Yuan, it is 394 kilometers from north to south and 144 kilometers across at its widest. The Tropic of Cancer, from Lesson 7, crosses it near Chiayi.": "76c9f528af17",
"From an airplane or a satellite, Taiwan's main island looks like a long spindle, wide in the middle and narrow at both ends. According to the Executive Yuan, it is 394 kilometers from north to south and 144 kilometers across at its widest. The Tropic of Cancer, from Lesson 7, crosses it near Chiayi. Most of the island is mountains. The Executive Yuan lists five ranges, from east to west: the Coastal Mountain Range, the Central Mountain Range, the Xueshan Range, the Yushan Range, and the Alishan Range. In the elevation data used on this page, about 31 out of every 100 squares of land are higher than 1,000 meters, and only about 29 out of 100 are lower than 100 meters. The highest peak is Yushan. In 2022, surveyors from the National Land Surveying and Mapping Center carried their instruments up the trail and measured its main peak at 3,952.433 meters, give or take 0.046 meters. The government's Taiwan portal counts 268 peaks higher than 3,000 meters. The mountains are not in the exact middle. Cut the island from west to east through Changhua: the highest point on that line, about 3,163 meters, is 96 kilometers from the west coast but only 30 kilometers from the east coast. So the east side is about three times as steep as the west side. On the east coast, the mountains drop almost straight into the sea. West of the Alishan Range, the land becomes gentler. From north to south, the Executive Yuan lists the Taipei Basin, the tablelands of Taoyuan, Hsinchu, and Miaoli, the Taichung Basin, the Chianan Plain, and the Pingtung Plain. In the east there is much less flat land: the narrow Huadong Longitudinal Valley lies between the Central and Coastal Mountain Ranges, and the Lanyang Plain opens to the sea in the northeast. This shape matters. The Central Mountain Range is the divide between the rivers that flow east and those that flow west. Because the west side is wider, its rivers are longer: the Zhuoshui River, Taiwan's longest at 186.6 kilometers, starts high in the mountains and reaches the sea between Changhua and Yunlin. And most of Taiwan's people live on the wide, gentle west side, as Lesson 8 would predict.": "216517719661",
